<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mursili I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 1595 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century-bce">16th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mursili I was an early king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>, remembered for his long-distance raid down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> around 1595 BCE. The campaign sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, carried off the statues of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> and his consort, and ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Babylonian Dynasty</a>. For the Hittite Empire it demonstrated a long operational reach, though it produced no lasting territorial gain, while for the Babylonian Empire it marked the final blow to the Old Babylonian state.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mursili I, also transcribed Mursilis or Murshili, was a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> and was likely a grandson of his predecessor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusili-i">Hattusili I</a>. His sister was Ḫarapšili and his wife was Queen Kali. He came to the throne as a minor, and on reaching adulthood he renewed Hattusili I's warfare in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. The dates of his reign depend on the chronology used: the middle chronology, the most widely accepted, places it from about 1620 to about 1590 BCE, and the short chronology from about 1556 to 1526 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Mursili invaded Yamhad and its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, which had eluded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusili-i">Hattusili I</a>. He then led a march of 2,000 km southeast into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, the first of its kind, where around 1595 BCE he sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. His motive is unclear. William Broad has proposed that he wanted grain, because clouds from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minoan-eruption">Minoan eruption</a> had reduced the Hittite harvests. The raid cannot have been meant to establish rule, since Babylon was too far from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. It is thought to have ended the Amorite dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> and allowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassites">Kassites</a> to take power, and may have arisen from an alliance with them or a wish to outdo his predecessor.</p><p>When Mursili returned to his kingdom he was assassinated in a conspiracy led by his brother-in-law Hantili I, who took the throne, and by Hantili's son-in-law Zidanta I. His death began a period of social unrest and decay of central rule, followed by the loss of the conquests made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mursili I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The final blow was the Hittite sack of Babylon around 1595 BCE, when King <b>Mursili I</b> led a long-distance raid down the Euphrates, took the city and carried off the cult statues of Marduk and his consort Zarpanitu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>These defences could not save it from a Hittite attack around 1600 BCE, probably by <b>Mursili I</b> or his predecessor Hattusili I.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ebla">Ebla · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His successor <b>Mursili I</b> raided Babylon around 1595 BCE and ended the First Babylonian Dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Circumstances favoured Mitanni's rise: the Hittite kingdom had fallen into turmoil after the murder of <b>Mursili I</b>, and Egypt was only beginning to reassert itself in the Levant after the Hyksos period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mitanni-empire">Mitanni Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Hittite king <b>Mursili I</b> sacked Babylon in 1595 BCE, Kassite rulers took over the city, perhaps first under Agum II, and around 1460 BCE Ulamburiash conquered the Sealand dynasty, uniting all Babylonia, called Karduniash.</blockquote><a href="/state/kassite-babylonia">Kassite Babylonia</a></li><li><blockquote>Hattusili I of the Hittite Empire attacked Yamhad, and <b>Mursili I</b> destroyed Aleppo c. 1595 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/yamhad">Yamhad</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusa">Hattusa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.02,34.62&amp;year=-1595" aria-label="Hattusa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Mursili I"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5302 4141h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5302 4141h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=40.02,34.62&amp;year=-1595">What was here in 1595 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
